package com.tracks.core.coaching

internal val COACHING_PARAGRAPH_FILES: List<Pair<String, String>> = listOf(
    "cardio.json" to "{\n  \"cardio.big_aerobic_base\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"Go long and easy on the {sport} today, {duration} min at {zone_desc}, conversational throughout; you're fresh (TSB {tsb:+.0f}), so it's cheap volume. Easy pace is where the engine gets built: it drives your slow-twitch fibers to grow more mitochondria, the cell's aerobic power plants, and lays down the capillaries that feed them oxygen. Staying under your first lactate threshold means you can bank hours of this without much recovery cost, which is why most of an endurance week should look exactly like it.\",\n      \"Bank an easy aerobic {sport} today, {duration} min at {zone_desc}. With your form sitting fresh (TSB {tsb:+.0f}) this is low-cost fitness: sustained easy efforts raise your plasma and stroke volume, so your heart moves more blood per beat, and they teach your muscles to burn fat instead of leaning on limited glycogen. Keep it genuinely conversational; going harder here trades a lot of fatigue for very little extra adaptation.\",\n      \"Head out for a relaxed {duration}-min {sport} at {zone_desc}. You're fresh (TSB {tsb:+.0f}), and the aerobic base you build at this pace is the foundation every faster session sits on: more mitochondria, denser capillaries, better fat use. It is deliberately unglamorous, and it is the single biggest driver of endurance, so resist the urge to push.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"Easy aerobic {sport}, {duration} min at {zone_desc}. You're fresh, so bank cheap volume; slow work builds the mitochondria and capillaries that carry your fitness.\",\n      \"Long and easy {sport}, {duration} min at {zone_desc}. Conversational pace grows the aerobic engine at low fatigue cost, which is why most of the week should look like this.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"cardio.tempo_productive\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"Do {duration} min on the {sport} today with the middle block at {zone_desc}, comfortably hard rather than a race. You're in the productive-fatigue range (TSB {tsb:+.0f}) with readiness holding at {readiness}, which is exactly when tempo work pays: sustained efforts near your lactate threshold push that threshold higher, so you can hold a faster pace before fatigue-causing lactate outruns your ability to clear it.\",\n      \"Run a tempo effort on the {sport}, {duration} min with a solid block at {zone_desc}. Sitting at TSB {tsb:+.0f} you can absorb the load, and threshold-pace work is the most direct lever on the pace you can sustain: it trains your body to shuttle and clear lactate faster, lifting the ceiling of your all-day effort. Keep it controlled; tempo is comfortably hard, not all-out.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"Tempo {sport}, {duration} min at {zone_desc}. You're in the productive range (TSB {tsb:+.0f}); threshold work lifts the pace you can hold before lactate builds.\",\n      \"Comfortably hard {sport}, {duration} min at {zone_desc}. Readiness is up and the fatigue is productive, so raise your lactate threshold today.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"cardio.quality_intervals\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"Do {duration} min with a hard interval block at {zone_desc} today; readiness is high ({readiness}) and you're carrying no fatigue, so this is the day to push. Three to five minute efforts spend the most time near your VO2max ceiling, and that is the specific stimulus that enlarges your heart's stroke volume and sharpens how much oxygen your muscles pull from the blood. It is potent and costly, so bank one hard day and keep everything around it genuinely easy.\",\n      \"Today suits intervals: {duration} min on the {sport} with hard reps at {zone_desc}. You're fresh (TSB {tsb:+.0f}) with readiness at {readiness}, and short, hard efforts raise your VO2max ceiling in a way easy miles simply cannot, driving central adaptations in the heart and peripheral ones in the muscle. One quality session is plenty; stack the rest of the week easy so you actually recover from it.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"Intervals, {duration} min with hard reps at {zone_desc}. You're fresh and ready ({readiness}); this is the session that raises your VO2max ceiling. Keep the rest of the week easy.\",\n      \"Hard {sport} intervals, {duration} min at {zone_desc}. No fatigue and high readiness make today the one to push; go hard here, easy everywhere else.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"cardio.easy_conversational\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"Keep it simple today: {duration} min easy on the {sport} at {zone_desc}. Nothing in your numbers says push (TSB {tsb:+.0f}, readiness {readiness}), and steady easy work is how you hold aerobic fitness without piling up fatigue you would have to recover from. Conversational pace, no heroics; the goal is a solid, repeatable session.\",\n      \"An easy {duration}-min {sport} at {zone_desc} is the right call. With readiness at {readiness} this is maintenance, not a test: easy aerobic work keeps your mitochondrial and capillary adaptations ticking over while leaving you fresh for the days that do call for intensity.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"Easy {sport}, {duration} min at {zone_desc}. Nothing says push today; steady aerobic work maintains fitness without adding fatigue.\",\n      \"Conversational {sport}, {duration} min at {zone_desc}. Keep it relaxed and repeatable; this is maintenance, not a test.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"cardio.recovery_flush\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"Keep today short and very easy: {duration} min on the {sport} at {zone_desc}. You put in a hard effort recently and your fatigue reflects it (TSB {tsb:+.0f}). Gentle movement actually speeds recovery, nudging blood through tired muscle to clear metabolic byproducts, whereas another hard day just deepens the hole. If it stops feeling easy, cut it short.\",\n      \"A recovery flush is the move: {duration} min of very easy {sport} at {zone_desc}. After a hard session, light aerobic work raises blood flow without adding meaningful training stress, which helps you turn the last effort into fitness rather than digging further into fatigue (TSB {tsb:+.0f}). Easy means easy here.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"Recovery flush, {duration} min very easy on the {sport} at {zone_desc}. Light movement clears fatigue faster than sitting still; keep it gentle.\",\n      \"Short and easy {sport}, {duration} min at {zone_desc}. You're carrying fatigue (TSB {tsb:+.0f}); flush it, don't add to it.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"cardio.cross_train_swap\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"Swap the routine today: {duration} min of easy {alt_sport} instead of another {sport} session. You've strung together {streak} sessions in the same sport, and that repeated, identical loading is where overuse niggles start. A different activity keeps the aerobic stimulus going while giving the specific tissues and joints from your usual sport a real break.\",\n      \"Try {duration} min of easy {alt_sport} today. Your recent training has leaned hard on one pattern, {streak} sessions of {sport} in a row, and mixing in another aerobic sport spreads the mechanical stress around while still building the same central, heart-and-lung fitness. Same engine, different wear.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"Easy {alt_sport}, {duration} min. You've done {streak} straight {sport} sessions; a different sport keeps the aerobic work but spreads the load.\",\n      \"Cross-train today: {duration} min easy {alt_sport}. Same aerobic benefit, less repetitive stress than more {sport}.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"cardio.rebuild_base\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"Get back to steady aerobic work today, {duration} min on the {sport} at {zone_desc}. Your form is very high (TSB {tsb:+.0f}), which usually means you've been training below what your body adapted to, and fitness fades in that gap: blood plasma volume can drop within days and your muscles' aerobic enzymes decline inside two to three weeks. Steady volume now arrests the slide before it costs you real conditioning.\",\n      \"Time to rebuild the base: {duration} min of aerobic {sport} at {zone_desc}. A TSB of {tsb:+.0f} is well past fresh and into detraining territory, where the adaptations you worked for start unwinding. The fix is not a hero session, it is a return to consistent, steady aerobic volume; start today.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"Steady aerobic {sport}, {duration} min at {zone_desc}. Your form is very high (TSB {tsb:+.0f}); fitness fades when load drops, so rebuild the base.\",\n      \"Aerobic {sport}, {duration} min at {zone_desc}. You're past fresh into detraining; consistent volume brings the engine back.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"cardio.consistency_nudge\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"Ease back in with {duration} min easy on the {sport} at {zone_desc}. It has been quiet lately, only {recent_count} sessions in the last two weeks, so don't overthink the first one back. Fitness responds to consistency far more than to any single hard workout; the win today is simply getting a repeatable, easy session on the board.\",\n      \"Get one easy session in today: {duration} min of {sport} at {zone_desc}. With only {recent_count} sessions in the past fortnight, the priority is rhythm, not intensity. Aerobic fitness is built by frequent, mostly easy work, so the habit matters more right now than the effort.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"Easy {sport}, {duration} min at {zone_desc}. Only {recent_count} sessions in two weeks; rebuild the habit, intensity can wait.\",\n      \"Short easy {sport}, {duration} min at {zone_desc}. Consistency drives fitness more than any single hard day; just get it in.\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n",
    "mobility.json" to "{\n  \"mobility.active_recovery\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"Swap today's session for {duration} min of easy mobility on {muscle_targets}, slow and static. Your fatigue is high (TSB {tsb:+.0f}) and readiness is low ({readiness}), and training only becomes fitness during recovery, when your body rebuilds above where it started. Pile hard work on top of deep fatigue and you just add damage without the resources to adapt, which is the road to overtraining and getting sick. Gentle mobility keeps blood moving without asking anything of that recovery.\",\n      \"Today is for recovery, not load: {duration} min of slow, static mobility on {muscle_targets}. With readiness at {readiness} and fatigue running high, the productive thing is to let your body catch up, since adaptation happens during rest rather than during the session. Easy mobility supports that by promoting blood flow to tired tissue without adding training stress.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"Easy mobility, {duration} min on {muscle_targets}. Fatigue is high and readiness low; recovery is where fitness is made, so don't add load today.\",\n      \"Gentle mobility, {duration} min. Slow and static on {muscle_targets}; let your body adapt instead of digging deeper.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"mobility.post_long_flush\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"After that last effort, do {duration} min of targeted mobility on {muscle_targets}. Gentle range-of-motion work moves things along faster than sitting still, easing the tissue that took the most load and helping you feel ready sooner. Hold each position long enough to actually let the muscle release rather than bouncing through it.\",\n      \"Follow up your recent long session with {duration} min of mobility focused on {muscle_targets}, the areas your {sport} loads most. Easy, deliberate stretching after hard work supports blood flow and comfortable range of motion without adding to the fatigue you're already clearing. Slow and unhurried is the point.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"Targeted mobility, {duration} min on {muscle_targets}. After a long effort, gentle range-of-motion work helps you bounce back faster than doing nothing.\",\n      \"Post-session mobility, {duration} min. Focus on {muscle_targets}; hold each position and let it release.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"mobility.neglected\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"Give {duration} min to mobility today, working {muscle_targets}. You haven't logged any lately, and it is the easiest thing to skip and the first thing to tighten up. Range of motion is quietly lost through repetitive training, and short, regular sessions preserve it far better than an occasional long one. Little and often is the whole trick.\",\n      \"Slot in a short mobility session, {duration} min on {muscle_targets}. Consistent training in the same patterns gradually shortens the muscles it uses, and without any counter-work your usable range slowly shrinks. A brief, regular session keeps your positions and stride from tightening over a block of training.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"Mobility, {duration} min on {muscle_targets}. It's easy to skip and quick to tighten up; short and regular beats occasional and long.\",\n      \"Short mobility session, {duration} min. Work {muscle_targets} to hold the range of motion your training quietly erodes.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"mobility.sport_specific_tightness\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"Spend {duration} min on mobility for {muscle_targets}, the areas your {sport} loads most. Repetitive sport-specific movement is exactly where range of motion quietly disappears, and keeping those muscles supple protects your stride or position from tightening as your training volume adds up. Slow, static holds work best.\",\n      \"Target {muscle_targets} with {duration} min of mobility today. Your {sport} works those muscles hard and through a limited range, so deliberately opening them back up keeps you moving efficiently and comfortably. Ease into each position and breathe rather than forcing it.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"Mobility for {muscle_targets}, {duration} min. Your {sport} loads these most; keeping them supple protects your stride and position.\",\n      \"Sport-specific mobility, {duration} min on {muscle_targets}. Static holds where your training tightens you up.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"mobility.rest_day_gentle\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"It's a rest day, so nothing is required. If you want to move, {duration} min of gentle mobility on {muscle_targets} is about the right dose: enough to feel better and keep blood flowing, not enough to count as a workout or eat into recovery.\",\n      \"No session needed today. Should you feel like moving, keep it to {duration} min of easy mobility on {muscle_targets}; light range-of-motion work supports recovery without adding any real load.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"Optional: {duration} min gentle mobility on {muscle_targets}. Enough to feel good on a rest day, not enough to count as training.\",\n      \"If you move at all, {duration} min easy mobility. Keep it light; today is for recovery.\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n",
    "onboarding.json" to "{\n  \"onboarding.no_history\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"There's not much history to work from yet, so treat this as a starting point rather than a tailored call. Pick one easy thing today, a {duration}-min walk, jog, or ride, and log it. Fitness is built far more by showing up consistently than by any single session, and a few logged workouts in, these recommendations start adapting to you.\",\n      \"Not enough logged yet for a personalized plan, so keep the first step simple: about {duration} min of easy movement you enjoy, then log it. Once there are a handful of sessions to read, this card will start matching your fatigue, your history, and where strength and mobility fit for you.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"Start simple: about {duration} min of easy movement, then log it. A few sessions in, these recommendations tailor to you.\",\n      \"Pick one easy thing, {duration} min, and log it. Consistency now; personalized guidance follows once there's history.\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n",
    "rest.json" to "{\n  \"rest.overdue\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"Take a full rest day. You've trained {streak} days in a row and the numbers are asking for a break (TSB {tsb:+.0f}). Nothing earns you more right now than the recovery will: fitness is built when you rest, not when you train, and unbroken loading eventually raises your risk of illness and injury more than it raises your fitness. A light walk is fine if you want to move, but no real session today.\",\n      \"Rest today, properly. A streak of {streak} straight training days with fatigue this high (TSB {tsb:+.0f}) is the point of diminishing returns; the adaptations you're chasing happen during recovery, and skipping it just accumulates strain. Give your body the day to turn recent work into fitness.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"Full rest day. {streak} days straight and fatigue is high; recovery is where the fitness gets made.\",\n      \"Rest today. You've trained {streak} days running; a light walk at most, nothing structured.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"rest.injury_risk_ramp\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"Take a full rest day, or very easy movement at most. Your fitness has jumped about {ramp:.0f} points a week, and here is the risk: your tendons and bones remodel much more slowly than your heart and lungs improve, so when load climbs faster than those tissues can keep up, soft-tissue injury risk rises sharply. One easy day lets the slower-adapting tissue catch up to the engine you've already built.\",\n      \"Back off today; rest or gentle movement only. A ramp of roughly {ramp:.0f} points a week means you're building load faster than your connective tissue can safely adapt, which is one of the more reliable precursors to overuse injury. Letting the ramp settle now protects the training you've banked.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"Rest day. Your load has ramped about {ramp:.0f} points a week, past where injury risk climbs; let the slower tissues catch up.\",\n      \"Ease off today. Fitness is rising faster than tendons and bones adapt; a rest day protects against overuse.\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n",
    "strength.json" to "{\n  \"strength.overdue\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"Slot in a {focus} strength session today, about {duration} min. You haven't lifted in {days_since} days, under the twice-a-week that keeps strength from sliding. After a session the muscle's protein-building machinery only stays elevated for a day or two before settling back to baseline, so training each area twice a week keeps you in that adaptive window far more of the time than one long session can. Spreading ten to twenty hard sets per muscle across the week is the dose the research points to, and it breaks up the repetitive stress of an all-cardio block.\",\n      \"Time for a {focus} strength day, roughly {duration} min. It has been {days_since} days since your last one, and strength is a use-it-or-lose-it quality: the signal to build fades within about 48 hours of a session, which is why consistency beats the occasional big effort. Two sessions a week, ten to twenty quality sets per muscle group, is the target worth defending.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"{focus} strength, about {duration} min. It's been {days_since} days; strength slips without two sessions a week, so get one in.\",\n      \"Strength day, {focus}, {duration} min. The build signal fades within about two days of a session; you're overdue at {days_since}.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"strength.heavy_fresh\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"You're fresh and recovered, so make it a heavy {focus} day, about {duration} min. Load the main lifts with lower reps and longer rest; heavy work recruits your high-threshold motor units, the ones that drive real strength gains, and doing it while fresh (TSB {tsb:+.0f}, readiness {readiness}) keeps both the quality and the safety high. Save the high-rep burnout sets for a more fatigued day.\",\n      \"Today suits heavy lifting: a {focus} session around {duration} min with challenging loads and full rest between sets. With readiness at {readiness} and no accumulated fatigue, your nervous system can express real force, which is exactly what heavy, low-rep work trains. Leave a couple of reps in reserve and let the rest periods do their job.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"Heavy {focus} strength, about {duration} min. You're fresh ({readiness}); low reps and long rest recruit the motor units that build strength.\",\n      \"Heavy day, {focus}, {duration} min. No fatigue means clean, safe force production; load the main lifts and rest fully.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"strength.posterior_caution\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"Point today's strength at your {focus}, about {duration} min. Your last big effort already taxed the posterior chain, so stacking heavy hinges or squats on top isn't ideal; muscle groups need roughly 48 hours to recover before they adapt rather than just accumulate damage. An upper-body session lets you keep training productively while your hamstrings, glutes, and lower back get that window.\",\n      \"Keep the load off the posterior chain today and train {focus} instead, {duration} min. That last session hit your hamstrings and lower back hard, and hitting them again before they've had about two days would cut into recovery rather than add to it. Push and pull for the upper body now; the legs get their turn once they've rebuilt.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"{focus} strength, {duration} min. Your last effort loaded the posterior chain; give it 48 hours and train upper body today.\",\n      \"Upper-body day, {duration} min. Avoid stacking heavy hinges on tired hamstrings; let them recover while you train elsewhere.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"strength.return_after_layoff\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"Ease back in today with a full-body session at moderate loads, stopping two reps short of failure, about {duration} min. It has been {days_since} days, so this is re-entry, not a test: the first session back always causes the most muscle damage and soreness, and your body then adapts so the next bout does far less. That protection is the repeated-bout effect, and it's why going too hard now just buys a wrecked week for no extra gain. Keep the next one light too.\",\n      \"Treat today as a restart: {focus}, moderate weights, {duration} min, well short of failure. After {days_since} days away, your muscles have lost some of their protective conditioning, so the first session will feel harder and leave you sorer than the load justifies. Go submaximal now and the repeated-bout effect makes the following sessions markedly smoother.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"Full-body restart, {duration} min, moderate and short of failure. It's been {days_since} days; the first session back is always the sorest, so ease in.\",\n      \"Easy re-entry strength, {duration} min. After {days_since} days off, go submaximal; the next session will hurt far less.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"strength.maintenance\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"Keep the rhythm going with a {focus} session today, about {duration} min. You're training strength consistently, and it's been {days_since} days since the last one, enough recovery to load the same patterns again productively. Holding two-plus quality sessions a week is what preserves the strength and tendon stiffness that support everything else you do.\",\n      \"You're on a good strength cadence, so bank another {focus} day, {duration} min. At {days_since} days since your last session the target muscles have recovered and are ready to adapt again. Consistency is the whole game here; two solid sessions a week keeps your hard-won strength from quietly eroding.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"{focus} strength, {duration} min. It's been {days_since} days and you're recovered; keep the twice-a-week rhythm.\",\n      \"Strength day, {focus}, {duration} min. You're on cadence; maintaining two sessions a week protects your strength base.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"strength.sport_support\": {\n    \"full\": [\n      \"Add two heavy strength sessions a week, starting today with {focus}, about {duration} min. Your training is almost all {sport}, and heavy lifting is one of the best-evidenced ways to get faster at endurance: it stiffens your tendons so they return more energy on each stride or pedal stroke, and recruits more muscle per contraction, improving your economy by a few percent. Because those gains are neural and tendon-driven rather than size-driven, a couple of sessions a week won't add weight or slow you down.\",\n      \"Bring some strength work into a mostly-{sport} routine, {focus}, {duration} min. Endurance athletes who lift heavy improve their movement economy, meaning every stride or stroke costs less oxygen, largely through stiffer tendons and better motor-unit recruitment rather than bigger muscles. Two focused sessions a week is the dose, and it pays off in your {sport}.\"\n    ],\n    \"compact\": [\n      \"Support strength, {focus}, {duration} min. Your training is almost all {sport}; heavy lifting improves economy through stiffer tendons, no added weight.\",\n      \"{focus} strength, {duration} min. Two heavy sessions a week make endurance cheaper, stride for stride.\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n",
)
